## Closing answer

"I define a RateLimiter interface with `allow(key)`. The default implementation is a token bucket per key, held in a ConcurrentHashMap and created with computeIfAbsent. Each bucket stores fractional tokens and the last refill time; `tryTake` adds tokens for the elapsed time, capped at capacity, then takes one if at least one is available – all inside one synchronized method, so two threads can never spend the same token, while different keys never block each other. Time comes from an injected nanosecond clock, so tests are instant. A sliding-window log is an alternative behind the same interface, and across servers the bucket moves to Redis with an atomic script."
